 "equations": [],
 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "What it does",
   "page": 2,
   "text": "A GaAs infrared LED is optically coupled to a high-speed detector IC with a Schmitt trigger and an open-collector output. With the LED lit past its threshold the output pulls low; dark, it is released and an external resistor takes it high. Built-in hysteresis gives noise immunity and pulse shaping, and the part is specified free of latch-up and oscillation across its voltage and temperature ranges."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Thresholds and grades",
   "page": 5,
   "text": "The three grades have different guaranteed turn-on currents: 1.6 mA for H11L1M, 5.0 mA for H11L3M and 10.0 mA for H11L2M. Turn-off happens at 0.50 to 0.90 of the turn-on current (0.75 typical), and at least 0.3 mA. Typical transfer: the output falls at about 1.0 mA and comes back at about 0.8 mA (Figure 3)."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Choosing the LED current",
   "page": 5,
   "text": "A 10% guard band above the grade's IF(on) maximum is recommended to cover LED degradation over life; the LED is limited to 30 mA continuous. The thresholds rise with temperature, about 1.3 times the 25 °C value at 100 °C and 0.8 times at -55 °C (Figure 5), and fall steeply below about 4.5 V of supply (Figure 4)."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Output",
   "page": 5,
   "text": "The output sinks 16 mA at 0.4 V maximum; VOL is 0.2 V typical, 0.4 V maximum with RL = 270 Ω from 5 V. VOL passes 0.5 V near 30 mA of load (Figure 6). Pin 4 is rated 0 to 16 V; leakage when off is 100 µA at most at VCC = VO = 15 V. Absolute limit 50 mA."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Supply",
   "page": 5,
   "text": "VCC runs from 3 V to 15 V (16 V absolute). Supply current is 1.6 mA typical, 5.0 mA maximum at 5 V, with the LED on or off; it grows with VCC and is higher with the LED lit (Figure 7)."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Speed",
   "page": 5,
   "text": "Turn-on 1.0 µs and turn-off 1.2 µs typical, 4.0 µs maximum each, with output edges of about 0.1 µs; 1 MHz NRZ typical data rate."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Isolation",
   "page": 3,
   "text": "Isolation is 4170 VACrms for one minute (UL1577). Parts with the V option are certified to DIN EN/IEC60747-5-5 at 850 V peak working voltage, 6000 V peak transient. External creepage and clearance are at least 7 mm, clearance at least 10 mm with option TV (0.4\" lead spacing); insulation is at least 0.5 mm thick."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Uses",
   "page": 2,
   "text": "Named uses: logic-to-logic isolation, programmable current level sensing, line receivers that reject noise and transients, squaring AC into TTL levels, digital programming of power supplies, and computer-to-peripheral interfaces."
  }
 ],
